1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,387
Your team deploys its hosted service
several times a day from main.

2
00:00:08,427 --> 00:00:13,194
The new manager's last company used develop,
release branches and hotfix branches,

3
00:00:13,234 --> 00:00:16,945
and the proposal arrives as a
slide with the well-known diagram.

4
00:00:16,985 --> 00:00:21,458
You have to answer in a meeting, and "I
prefer trunk-based development" is an

5
00:00:21,498 --> 00:00:22,806
opinion, not an answer.

6
00:00:23,146 --> 00:00:27,900
By the end of this video you can say what
Git Flow prescribes, commit by commit,

7
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:32,514
what its own author wrote about it in
2020, and what each of the other models

8
00:00:32,554 --> 00:00:36,588
assumes about how software is
delivered. None of them is a standard.

9
00:00:36,628 --> 00:00:41,382
Each is an answer to a delivery situation.
Keep the words "industry standard" in

10
00:00:41,422 --> 00:00:44,677
mind. The author of that
diagram has a comment on them.

11
00:00:46,517 --> 00:00:50,406
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:50,446 --> 00:00:54,592
Three words first. A commit is one
saved snapshot of the project.

13
00:00:54,632 --> 00:00:59,423
A branch is a name that points at a
commit and moves forward as you commit.

14
00:00:59,463 --> 00:01:02,257
A tag is a name that
isn't expected to move.

15
00:01:02,597 --> 00:01:07,889
In video 170 the workflow involved two
servers. Today everything is inside one

16
00:01:07,929 --> 00:01:13,494
repository, and the subject is which branches
exist and how commits travel between

17
00:01:13,534 --> 00:01:13,836
them.

18
00:01:14,176 --> 00:01:19,647
Chapter 27 reduces the subject to one
sentence: a branching strategy is nothing but

19
00:01:19,687 --> 00:01:24,826
an agreement about which refs exist, who
may move them, and in which direction

20
00:01:24,866 --> 00:01:30,005
commits travel between them. You know
from video 36 what --no-ff does and what

21
00:01:30,045 --> 00:01:35,516
first-parent history is: --no-ff makes a
merge commit even where a fast-forward was

22
00:01:35,556 --> 00:01:40,762
possible, and first-parent history follows
only the first parent of each merge.

23
00:01:40,802 --> 00:01:46,273
You know from video 82 that a release is a
tag and that git describe and tag ranges

24
00:01:46,313 --> 00:01:47,999
answer "what did we ship".

25
00:01:48,339 --> 00:01:53,440
We go through five things: branch names,
GitHub Flow, Git Flow with a replayed

26
00:01:53,480 --> 00:01:58,581
history, the 2020 note, and then GitLab
Flow, trunk-based development, release

27
00:01:58,621 --> 00:02:02,206
branches and Microsoft's
Release Flow, each as a graph.

28
00:02:04,046 --> 00:02:05,624
Here's what you'll walk away with.

29
00:02:05,964 --> 00:02:07,376
After this video you can:

30
00:02:07,716 --> 00:02:12,853
Explain why main, develop, feature/*,
hotfix/* and release/* are conventions and

31
00:02:12,893 --> 00:02:14,407
what gives a name force.

32
00:02:14,747 --> 00:02:17,808
Describe GitHub Flow and what
it assumes about deployment.

33
00:02:18,148 --> 00:02:22,216
Describe Git Flow and state what
its author wrote about it in 2020.

34
00:02:22,556 --> 00:02:25,606
Describe trunk-based development
and release branches.

35
00:02:25,946 --> 00:02:29,665
Draw the graph each model produces
for one release and one hotfix.

36
00:02:31,505 --> 00:02:37,226
Branch names are conventions, not laws.
main, develop, feature/*, bugfix/*, hotfix/*

37
00:02:37,266 --> 00:02:42,712
and release/* are names that people agreed
on. Git gives none of them a meaning.

38
00:02:43,052 --> 00:02:49,091
To Git, refs/heads/release/1.4 is a ref
whose name happens to contain a slash.

39
00:02:49,131 --> 00:02:55,482
The slash matters in exactly two technical
ways. It lets patterns select groups of

40
00:02:55,522 --> 00:03:01,640
branches: git branch --list 'release/*',
a ruleset target such as release/**, a

41
00:03:01,680 --> 00:03:08,109
workflow filter such as branches: ['release/**'].
And in the files backend it makes

42
00:03:08,149 --> 00:03:14,578
release a directory, so a branch named
release and a branch named release/1.4 can't

43
00:03:14,618 --> 00:03:15,435
both exist.

44
00:03:15,775 --> 00:03:21,129
Read the right-hand column. Force comes
from three places: a platform setting, a

45
00:03:21,169 --> 00:03:26,725
rule with a pattern, which GitHub calls a
ruleset, or automation that triggers on a

46
00:03:26,765 --> 00:03:30,837
pattern. Everything else is
habit. The lab configuration sets

47
00:03:30,877 --> 00:03:35,422
init.defaultBranch=main. Unconfigured
Git 2.55 still creates master.

48
00:03:35,462 --> 00:03:38,523
Neither name does anything
the other does not.

49
00:03:38,863 --> 00:03:43,653
Quick quiz. What gives the name
develop its force? A, Git itself.

50
00:03:43,693 --> 00:03:47,518
B, a platform setting. C,
only the team's agreement.

51
00:03:47,558 --> 00:03:48,409
Your answer?

52
00:03:53,249 --> 00:03:57,390
C, only the team's agreement. That
was the second row of the table.

53
00:03:57,730 --> 00:04:01,586
The convention earns its keep
when rules are attached to it.

54
00:04:01,626 --> 00:04:06,846
"Nobody pushes to release/** except through
a pull request with two approvals" is

55
00:04:06,886 --> 00:04:12,041
one ruleset with one pattern, and it works
only if every release branch is named

56
00:04:12,081 --> 00:04:17,236
that way. A name that carries the author,
such as asha/rerun, says the branch is

57
00:04:17,276 --> 00:04:18,990
private and may be rebased.

58
00:04:19,330 --> 00:04:25,180
Try it now, for thirty seconds. In any
repository you have, type git branch --list

59
00:04:25,220 --> 00:04:30,927
'release/*'. It only reads. It lists the
branches whose names match the pattern.

60
00:04:30,967 --> 00:04:31,645
I'll wait.

61
00:04:36,485 --> 00:04:40,960
If it printed nothing, no branch of yours
matches. That's the point of a naming

62
00:04:41,000 --> 00:04:44,274
pattern: a branch outside the
pattern is outside the rule.

63
00:04:44,614 --> 00:04:49,755
GitHub Flow. One long-lived branch that is
always deployable, short branches off it,

64
00:04:49,795 --> 00:04:53,578
a pull request for each, and
deployment right after the merge.

65
00:04:53,618 --> 00:04:58,512
Deployable means that it can go into
service for users at any moment, and a pull

66
00:04:58,552 --> 00:05:02,397
request is GitHub's object for
proposing and reviewing a merge.

67
00:05:02,737 --> 00:05:07,810
GitHub's documentation describes six steps:
create a branch, make changes, create a

68
00:05:07,850 --> 00:05:11,753
pull request, address review
comments, merge, delete the branch.

69
00:05:11,793 --> 00:05:16,743
Scott Chacon's 2011 essay, where the name
comes from, adds the rules that give it

70
00:05:16,783 --> 00:05:21,918
its character: anything on the main branch
is deployable, and you deploy immediately

71
00:05:21,958 --> 00:05:22,780
after merging.

72
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:28,194
He also gives the reason GitHub didn't use
Git Flow: GitHub deployed many times a

73
00:05:28,234 --> 00:05:33,308
day, while Git Flow is organised around
releases. Martin Fowler's reading is that

74
00:05:33,348 --> 00:05:38,170
the model assumes a single production
version, so release branches and hotfix

75
00:05:38,210 --> 00:05:41,579
branches disappear: a hotfix
is one more short branch.

76
00:05:41,919 --> 00:05:46,794
What it assumes: that there is one live
version, that main can be deployed at any

77
00:05:46,834 --> 00:05:51,890
commit, and that a broken deployment is
repaired by rolling forward or back quickly.

78
00:05:51,930 --> 00:05:56,684
Microsoft's description of its own practice
records where the strict form stops

79
00:05:56,724 --> 00:06:01,599
scaling: when a repository completes more
than 200 pull requests a day, deploying

80
00:06:01,639 --> 00:06:06,635
each one before or after merge turns into
a queue, so Microsoft batches deployments

81
00:06:06,675 --> 00:06:08,394
into sprint releases instead.

82
00:06:08,734 --> 00:06:13,108
What it doesn't say: how long a feature
branch may live, or what to do when a

83
00:06:13,148 --> 00:06:17,923
customer can't take the newest version. The
next video shows that consequence with a

84
00:06:17,963 --> 00:06:18,669
real history.

85
00:06:19,009 --> 00:06:23,513
Git Flow. Two long-lived branches,
main for released code and develop for

86
00:06:23,553 --> 00:06:28,493
integration, plus three kinds of supporting
branch: feature, release and hotfix.

87
00:06:28,833 --> 00:06:33,731
Vincent Driessen published "A successful
Git branching model" on the fifth of

88
00:06:33,771 --> 00:06:38,926
January 2010. Feature branches start from
and merge into develop, with --no-ff so

89
00:06:38,966 --> 00:06:44,056
that each feature stays visible as a unit.
A release branch is cut from develop,

90
00:06:44,096 --> 00:06:49,315
receives only stabilization commits, and
is merged into main, and tagged, and back

91
00:06:49,355 --> 00:06:54,702
into develop. A hotfix branch is cut from
main, and is merged into main, and tagged,

92
00:06:54,742 --> 00:06:55,792
and into develop.

93
00:06:56,132 --> 00:07:01,338
The 2020 note. On the fifth of March 2020
the author added a "note of reflection" at

94
00:07:01,378 --> 00:07:06,583
the top of the article. I give it as the
course's research report summarizes it, not

95
00:07:06,623 --> 00:07:11,454
as a quotation, in three points. First,
the model was conceived for explicitly

96
00:07:11,494 --> 00:07:15,951
versioned software that may need
several versions supported in the wild.

97
00:07:16,291 --> 00:07:21,249
Second, a team doing continuous delivery of
a web application should adopt a simpler

98
00:07:21,289 --> 00:07:26,247
workflow such as GitHub Flow. Third, readers
should weigh their own context, because

99
00:07:26,287 --> 00:07:30,828
no model is a cure-all. Fowler adds that
Git Flow says nothing about how long

100
00:07:30,868 --> 00:07:35,291
feature branches live, and Atlassian's
tutorial now labels Gitflow a legacy

101
00:07:35,331 --> 00:07:35,826
workflow.

102
00:07:36,166 --> 00:07:40,899
"Use Git Flow" as a default for every
project. The author of the model restricts it

103
00:07:40,939 --> 00:07:44,637
to explicitly versioned software
with several supported versions.

104
00:07:44,677 --> 00:07:49,122
For a continuously deployed service it
adds a second long-lived branch and two

105
00:07:49,162 --> 00:07:52,917
merges per release, and the tags
already record what was released.

106
00:07:52,957 --> 00:07:57,058
That's the author's comment on "the
industry standard" from the opening.

107
00:07:57,398 --> 00:08:02,297
When it still fits. Installed software,
firmware, SDKs and libraries with several

108
00:08:02,337 --> 00:08:07,054
maintained versions, and organizations
where a release is a scheduled, audited

109
00:08:07,094 --> 00:08:12,115
event. Even there, the part that does the
work is the release branch, which you can

110
00:08:12,155 --> 00:08:13,396
have without develop.

111
00:08:13,736 --> 00:08:18,536
GitLab Flow. GitLab defines it as a
simplified strategy that works directly with

112
00:08:18,576 --> 00:08:23,255
main and adds, where needed, a production
branch, environment branches such as

113
00:08:23,295 --> 00:08:27,914
staging and production, or release
branches for software shipped in versions.

114
00:08:27,954 --> 00:08:32,754
Three of its eleven published rules: fix
bugs in main first and release branches

115
00:08:32,794 --> 00:08:36,565
second, base releases on tags,
and never rebase pushed commits.

116
00:08:36,905 --> 00:08:41,222
GitLab's original 2014 "GitLab Flow"
document is no longer reachable on

117
00:08:41,262 --> 00:08:46,132
docs.gitlab.com. The phrase "upstream
first" for its fix direction was read only

118
00:08:46,172 --> 00:08:50,121
through a mirror, according to the
research notes of this course.

119
00:08:50,161 --> 00:08:54,539
The two current pages the textbook
links are the source for what I said.

120
00:08:54,879 --> 00:08:59,744
Trunk-based development. Developers integrate
into one branch, the trunk, and avoid

121
00:08:59,784 --> 00:09:04,589
other long-lived development branches. Very
small teams may commit straight to the

122
00:09:04,629 --> 00:09:09,553
trunk. Larger teams use short-lived feature
branches, which should last no more than

123
00:09:09,593 --> 00:09:14,516
a couple of days, belong to one developer
or a pair, and be deleted after the merge.

124
00:09:14,856 --> 00:09:19,767
Long-running changes are handled with feature
flags and a technique called branch by

125
00:09:19,807 --> 00:09:24,482
abstraction, instead of long branches. A
feature flag is a condition in the code

126
00:09:24,522 --> 00:09:29,373
that keeps unfinished behavior switched
off. Fowler treats it as close to a synonym

127
00:09:29,413 --> 00:09:30,965
for continuous integration.

128
00:09:31,305 --> 00:09:35,040
The difference from GitHub Flow
is one of degree, not of kind.

129
00:09:35,080 --> 00:09:39,850
Both have one long-lived branch. Trunk-based
development adds a limit on branch

130
00:09:39,890 --> 00:09:44,052
lifetime and, in its strictest form,
drops the pre-merge review gate.

131
00:09:44,392 --> 00:09:49,140
Release branches. A release branch is cut
from the trunk shortly before a release,

132
00:09:49,180 --> 00:09:53,636
receives only fixes, and is the place
the release and its patches are tagged.

133
00:09:53,676 --> 00:09:57,139
The trunk-based development site
states the rules: cut late.

134
00:09:57,179 --> 00:10:00,818
The branch may start from a commit
older than the trunk's head.

135
00:10:01,158 --> 00:10:05,527
It's never merged back. It's deleted
when the version is no longer supported.

136
00:10:05,567 --> 00:10:09,937
And bugs are reproduced and fixed on the
trunk first and cherry-picked to the

137
00:10:09,977 --> 00:10:14,575
release branch. A cherry-pick copies one
commit's change onto another branch as a

138
00:10:14,615 --> 00:10:16,007
new commit with a new ID.

139
00:10:16,347 --> 00:10:21,788
Microsoft's Release Flow. Short-lived topic
branches are merged to main through pull

140
00:10:21,828 --> 00:10:27,270
requests with branch policies. At the end
of each three-week sprint a release branch

141
00:10:27,310 --> 00:10:32,099
is created, named for the sprint. The
document's example is releases/M129.

142
00:10:32,139 --> 00:10:37,581
It's deployed in rings. A hotfix is made in
main first and then cherry-picked to the

143
00:10:37,621 --> 00:10:40,452
release branch through
its own pull request.

144
00:10:40,792 --> 00:10:45,612
Release branches never merge back to main,
and the old one is abandoned after the

145
00:10:45,652 --> 00:10:50,652
next sprint ships. Release Flow is trunk-based
development plus a release branch per

146
00:10:50,692 --> 00:10:55,631
sprint plus the "fix on main first" rule.
The names differ more than the graphs do.

147
00:10:57,471 --> 00:11:02,113
Now one question that sorts them all.
All of these models keep one integration

148
00:11:02,153 --> 00:11:06,734
branch. They differ in what, if anything,
sits downstream of it, and in which

149
00:11:06,774 --> 00:11:08,174
direction a fix travels.

150
00:11:08,514 --> 00:11:12,419
Use this as the sorting question:
how many versions are alive at once?

151
00:11:12,459 --> 00:11:16,816
If the answer is one, there's nothing
downstream of main, and you're somewhere

152
00:11:16,856 --> 00:11:21,325
between GitHub Flow and trunk-based
development. If the answer is more than one,

153
00:11:21,365 --> 00:11:26,004
something has to name each live version,
and that something is a release branch or,

154
00:11:26,044 --> 00:11:28,371
in Git Flow, the pair of main and develop.

155
00:11:28,711 --> 00:11:33,426
A way to picture it: a river and its canals.
The integration branch is the river.

156
00:11:33,466 --> 00:11:38,122
A release branch is a canal cut from the
river at one point, with its own slower

157
00:11:38,162 --> 00:11:42,878
water. The models differ in whether canals
exist and whether water is ever pumped

158
00:11:42,918 --> 00:11:44,815
from a canal back into the river.

159
00:11:45,155 --> 00:11:48,534
Where the picture breaks: water
mixes, and commits do not.

160
00:11:48,574 --> 00:11:53,308
A fix that is cherry-picked into a canal
is a second commit with a second ID, and

161
00:11:53,348 --> 00:11:57,199
Git records no link between the
two. The next video is about that.

162
00:11:59,039 --> 00:12:03,180
A question for you: what is
between the two tags on main?

163
00:12:03,220 --> 00:12:08,095
The hotfix, and also the merge M3.
Hold that thought for video 172.

164
00:12:08,435 --> 00:12:13,880
Now the four diagrams of section 27.8 again.
GitLab Flow with a production branch:

165
00:12:13,920 --> 00:12:18,964
every merge deploys to staging, and a
merge from main deploys to production.

166
00:12:19,304 --> 00:12:22,488
Trunk-based development: branches
that live hours to two days.

167
00:12:22,828 --> 00:12:26,987
A release branch, with the fix made on
main first, and its cherry-pick on the

168
00:12:27,027 --> 00:12:27,369
branch.

169
00:12:27,709 --> 00:12:32,748
Microsoft's Release Flow: the
hotfix is cherry-picked to M129.

170
00:12:33,088 --> 00:12:38,061
Put the last two side by side: it's the same
graph, with a branch per version in one

171
00:12:38,101 --> 00:12:40,268
and a branch per sprint in the other.

172
00:12:42,108 --> 00:12:47,431
Into the lab. The project is the gateway
promptgate, arranged as Git Flow

173
00:12:47,471 --> 00:12:52,132
prescribes: features are merged
into develop, each with --no-ff.

174
00:12:52,472 --> 00:12:57,820
Step 1: the release branch. 🟡 CAUTION:
git merge moves the current branch.

175
00:12:57,860 --> 00:13:00,587
--no-ff always creates a merge commit.

176
00:13:00,927 --> 00:13:05,732
Predict: how many merge commits does one
release create, and on which branches?

177
00:13:05,772 --> 00:13:06,714
Say it out loud.

178
00:13:11,554 --> 00:13:17,439
Two: one on main, which is tagged, and
one on develop, so that develop has the

179
00:13:17,479 --> 00:13:21,465
version bump too. The release
branch is then deleted.

180
00:13:21,505 --> 00:13:25,416
Its tip 7237206 stays
reachable through both merges.

181
00:13:28,756 --> 00:13:34,247
Step 2: the hotfix. It starts from main,
which is what production runs, not from

182
00:13:34,287 --> 00:13:34,801
develop.

183
00:13:35,141 --> 00:13:39,674
Again two merges. The fix
itself is one commit, 1607dca.

184
00:13:40,014 --> 00:13:45,420
Step 3: the graph. 🟢 SAFE. Predict before
it prints: where will you find the hotfix

185
00:13:45,460 --> 00:13:48,241
commit, and how many
children will it have?

186
00:13:53,081 --> 00:13:58,313
Take your time. This graph is hard to
read, and that's part of the lesson.

187
00:13:58,353 --> 00:14:01,733
Find 1607dca, and count
its children. I'll wait.

188
00:14:06,573 --> 00:14:12,406
It has two children: the merge a3de55c on
main, tagged v1.4.1, and the merge 24124ab

189
00:14:12,446 --> 00:14:18,070
on develop. That double merge is the model's
answer to "how does a fix reach both

190
00:14:18,110 --> 00:14:23,314
lines". It's a merge-upward answer: one
commit, one ID, reachable from both

191
00:14:23,354 --> 00:14:23,944
branches.

192
00:14:27,284 --> 00:14:28,930
Step 4: what shipped.

193
00:14:29,270 --> 00:14:34,706
Four readings. The patch release contains
the fix and nothing else, because main

194
00:14:34,746 --> 00:14:40,319
receives only releases and hotfixes. git
log --first-parent main is a release log,

195
00:14:40,359 --> 00:14:45,727
one line per release. The history between
v1.3.0 and develop contains six merge

196
00:14:45,767 --> 00:14:49,493
commits for three features,
one release and one hotfix.

197
00:14:49,533 --> 00:14:54,901
And the second parent of the hotfix merge,
the fix itself, is contained in both

198
00:14:54,941 --> 00:14:56,064
develop and main.

199
00:14:56,404 --> 00:15:01,190
So the model delivers what it promises: a
clean patch release and a readable release

200
00:15:01,230 --> 00:15:03,258
log. The price is on the third line.

201
00:15:05,098 --> 00:15:06,746
Five mistakes to watch for.

202
00:15:07,086 --> 00:15:11,919
Treating a branch name as a control. Root
cause: Git gives no meaning to a name;

203
00:15:11,959 --> 00:15:16,609
only a platform setting, a ruleset pattern
or automation that triggers on the

204
00:15:16,649 --> 00:15:18,009
pattern gives it force.

205
00:15:18,349 --> 00:15:21,435
Adopting Git Flow for a
continuously deployed service.

206
00:15:21,475 --> 00:15:26,180
Root cause: the model was conceived for
explicitly versioned software with several

207
00:15:26,220 --> 00:15:30,983
supported versions, and a service with one
live version has nothing for develop and

208
00:15:31,023 --> 00:15:32,662
release branches to separate.

209
00:15:33,002 --> 00:15:36,357
Calling GitHub Flow and
trunk-based development opposites.

210
00:15:36,397 --> 00:15:41,041
Root cause: both have one long-lived
branch; the difference is a limit on branch

211
00:15:41,081 --> 00:15:44,729
lifetime and, in the strictest
form, the pre-merge review gate.

212
00:15:45,069 --> 00:15:50,142
Forgetting the merge back in Git Flow. Root
cause: a release or hotfix lives on two

213
00:15:50,182 --> 00:15:55,008
long-lived branches, and the model needs
a second merge to carry it to develop;

214
00:15:55,048 --> 00:15:56,671
nothing in Git reminds you.

215
00:15:57,011 --> 00:16:01,801
Naming a release branch inconsistently.
Root cause: rules and deployment workflows

216
00:16:01,841 --> 00:16:06,514
select branches by pattern, so a branch
outside the pattern is outside the rule.

217
00:16:08,354 --> 00:16:13,444
Now, out of the lab. A company runs an LLM
gateway as a hosted service and deploys

218
00:16:13,484 --> 00:16:18,136
from main after every merge. It uses
GitHub Flow and has never needed more.

219
00:16:18,176 --> 00:16:22,891
Then two enterprise customers sign
contracts to run the gateway on their own

220
00:16:22,931 --> 00:16:28,084
hardware and to stay one version behind.
This is the first question of the chapter:

221
00:16:28,124 --> 00:16:30,462
does the branching model survive that?

222
00:16:30,802 --> 00:16:35,798
The staff engineer doesn't propose Git
Flow. She applies the sorting question.

223
00:16:35,838 --> 00:16:40,060
Until now one version was alive.
Next quarter there will be three.

224
00:16:40,100 --> 00:16:45,226
Something has to name the two older ones,
so the team adds release branches, cut

225
00:16:45,266 --> 00:16:50,456
from main late, named by one pattern,
release/<version>, with one ruleset on that

226
00:16:50,496 --> 00:16:50,973
pattern.

227
00:16:51,313 --> 00:16:56,124
main stays the only integration branch,
and no develop is introduced, because the

228
00:16:56,164 --> 00:17:00,616
hosted service still deploys from main
and the tags already record what was

229
00:17:00,656 --> 00:17:05,587
released. She writes the scheme into
CONTRIBUTING.md, including the direction a fix

230
00:17:05,627 --> 00:17:08,462
travels, which is the
subject of the next video.

231
00:17:10,302 --> 00:17:17,490
Your turn. Do Exercise 32.1, Level 1,
"Names and what gives them force", in

232
00:17:17,530 --> 00:17:20,382
exercises/m32-m34-practice.md.

233
00:17:20,722 --> 00:17:25,723
Before you answer each item, predict in one
line what Git itself does with the name

234
00:17:25,763 --> 00:17:30,705
in question, and then what, if anything,
on the platform or in automation gives it

235
00:17:30,745 --> 00:17:35,503
force. Check your answers against the
table of section 27.5 only after you have

236
00:17:35,543 --> 00:17:36,536
written them all.

237
00:17:36,876 --> 00:17:42,637
The challenge is Exercise 32.2, Level 2,
"Six teams, which model?", in the same

238
00:17:42,677 --> 00:17:43,004
file.

239
00:17:46,844 --> 00:17:51,796
Answer out loud. The question has three
parts, and a strong answer gives each its

240
00:17:51,836 --> 00:17:56,910
own paragraph. The first is a precise
description: which long-lived branches, which

241
00:17:56,950 --> 00:18:01,347
supporting branches, which merges in
which direction, where the tags go.

242
00:18:01,387 --> 00:18:06,462
The second reports the author's 2020 note
accurately, as a restriction on where the

243
00:18:06,502 --> 00:18:08,434
model fits, not as a retraction.

244
00:18:08,774 --> 00:18:13,522
The third is a proposal that starts from
the team's delivery situation, says what

245
00:18:13,562 --> 00:18:17,543
the proposal assumes, and says on
which day it would have to change.

246
00:18:17,583 --> 00:18:22,509
Do it without contempt for the proposal.
The manager's last company may have shipped

247
00:18:22,549 --> 00:18:23,632
versioned software.

248
00:18:25,472 --> 00:18:27,521
Let's land this, in your own words.

249
00:18:27,861 --> 00:18:32,588
A branch name means nothing to Git; a
default-branch setting, a ruleset pattern or a

250
00:18:32,628 --> 00:18:34,348
workflow filter gives it force.

251
00:18:34,688 --> 00:18:39,624
GitHub Flow is one always-deployable branch
with short branches and deployment after

252
00:18:39,664 --> 00:18:41,934
merge, and it assumes one live version.

253
00:18:42,274 --> 00:18:47,483
Git Flow is main plus develop plus feature,
release and hotfix branches with merges

254
00:18:47,523 --> 00:18:52,796
in two directions; its author restricted it
in 2020 to explicitly versioned software

255
00:18:52,836 --> 00:18:54,820
with several supported versions.

256
00:18:55,160 --> 00:19:00,212
Trunk-based development limits branch lifetime
to a couple of days; release branches

257
00:19:00,252 --> 00:19:04,699
are cut late from the trunk, receive
only fixes and are never merged back.

258
00:19:05,039 --> 00:19:09,189
The question that sorts the models is
how many versions are alive at once.

259
00:19:11,029 --> 00:19:16,437
Read sections 27.5 to 27.8. Then read git
help workflows, the Git project's own

260
00:19:16,477 --> 00:19:22,023
workflow document, which the chapter cites.
Note what it says about the branch on

261
00:19:22,063 --> 00:19:27,540
which a fix is committed. You'll need that
sentence in the next video, where one

262
00:19:27,580 --> 00:19:31,126
release and one hotfix are
run under two strategies.

263
00:19:31,466 --> 00:19:36,119
Today you turned five well-known names
into five graphs, and you can answer that

264
00:19:36,159 --> 00:19:40,753
meeting with reasons instead of a preference.
Draw each graph once from memory.

265
00:19:40,793 --> 00:19:45,387
